{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":86970,"num_turns":2,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"4e8c52d5-e885-5d3f-95cb-936fc60f6c68","total_cost_usd":0.643806,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11482,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":8171,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11482,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8171,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11482,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11482},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":5511,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.005596,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":8171,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11482,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.6382099999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\":\"actor:dependence-free-batch-f1-reviewer-fable-28\",\"reviews\":[{\"case_id\":\"case:99fa42046e8fc8cc47de\",\"verdict\":\"demonstrated_issue\",\"issue_class\":\"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\",\"bounded_statement\":\"Within the supplied files only: the declared independent unit is the mesocosm (mesocosm_id, 12 units, 6 per substrate group), and each mesocosm contributes exactly four repeated outflow samples to data/input.csv. The workflow pools all 48 rows into two groups of 24 and applies scipy.stats.ttest_ind (Welch's two-sample t-test), a procedure whose validity requires each observation to be an independent unit. Each mesocosm therefore enters the row-independent test four times, so the reported statistics (t = -20.228, df = 45.02, p < 1e-06 on 48 'samples') overstate the effective sample size (48 instead of 12) and are not supported by the design. The bounded claim is about the inflated test statistics and degrees of freedom; a correctly aggregated per-mesocosm analysis (n = 6 vs 6 mesocosm means, computable from the report's own per-mesocosm table) would still find a significant difference in the same direction, so the qualitative conclusion of lower zinc under biochar is likely robust even though the quoted inferential quantities are not.\",\"root_cause\":\"The analysis code builds group_values by appending the zinc value of every row (every mesocosm-by-week sample) directly into the substrate group's list, then passes those pooled per-row lists to stats.ttest_ind. No aggregation to one value per mesocosm_id is performed before the inferential step, despite the data description designating mesocosm_id as the independent-unit column and stating that treatment was assigned to whole mesocosms and that samples from the same mesocosm share packing, planting, and hydraulic history. Repeated measures from the same independent unit are thus treated as independent replicates (pseudoreplication), inflating n from 12 to 48 and df from ~10 to 45.02.\",\"evidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The data description explicitly names mesocosm_id as the independent-unit column and defines a row as a single repeated water sample from a mesocosm.\",\"observed_value\":\"Independent unit = mesocosm_id; one row = one outflow sample from one mesocosm on one week\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":12,\"end_line\":13,\"quoted_text\":\"One row is: one outflow water sample taken from one mesocosm on one sampling week\\nIndependent unit column: mesocosm_id\"}]},{\"description\":\"The data description states that treatment was assigned to whole mesocosms and that within-mesocosm samples share the same physical history, i.e., rows from the same mesocosm are not independent.\",\"observed_value\":\"Treatment assigned at mesocosm level; within-mesocosm samples share packing, planting, hydraulic history\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":27,\"end_line\":30,\"quoted_text\":\"The file holds 48 rows, which is 12 mesocosms times 4 sampling weeks. The\\nsubstrate treatment was assigned to whole mesocosms, never to individual water\\nsamples, and samples drawn from the same mesocosm share its packing, its\\nplanting and its hydraulic history.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow appends every row's zinc value into the substrate group list with no per-mesocosm aggregation, so each mesocosm's four repeated samples all enter the group vectors.\",\"observed_value\":\"group_values[substrate].append(zinc) executed once per row (48 times)\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":63,\"end_line\":69,\"quoted_text\":\"for mesocosm_id, substrate, _week, zinc in samples:\\n        if mesocosm_id not in mesocosm_values:\\n            mesocosm_order.append(mesocosm_id)\\n            mesocosm_substrate[mesocosm_id] = substrate\\n            mesocosm_values[mesocosm_id] = []\\n        mesocosm_values[mesocosm_id].append(zinc)\\n        group_values[substrate].append(zinc)\"}]},{\"description\":\"The pooled per-row vectors (24 rows per group) are passed to a two-sample t-test, a row-independent procedure that assumes each observation is an independent unit.\",\"observed_value\":\"stats.ttest_ind on 24-vs-24 per-row values\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":80,\"end_line\":83,\"quoted_text\":\"outcome = stats.ttest_ind(values_a, values_b, equal_var=False)\\n    t_stat = float(outcome.statistic)\\n    p_value = float(outcome.pvalue)\\n    df = welch_df(sd_a ** 2, len(values_a), sd_b ** 2, len(values_b))\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report explicitly states that each outflow water sample (row) enters the test as one observation, and reports df = 45.02, consistent with n = 48 rows rather than n = 12 mesocosms.\",\"observed_value\":\"48 observations, Welch df = 45.02\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":39,\"end_line\":45,\"quoted_text\":\"Welch's two-sample t-test (two-sided, unequal variances) compares the\\ndissolved zinc concentration of the two substrate builds. Each outflow\\nwater sample in data/input.csv enters the test as one observation.\\n\\n- Mean difference (biochar_amended - gravel_control): -0.3800 mg/L\\n- Welch t = -20.228 on df = 45.02\\n- p < 1e-06\"}]},{\"description\":\"The selected conclusion bases its significance claim on the 48-row test, quoting the inflated t, df, and p as support.\",\"observed_value\":\"t = -20.228, df = 45.02, p < 1e-06 on 48 samples\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":50,\"end_line\":50,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Welch's two-sample t-test on 48 outflow water samples: mean dissolved zinc is 0.3800 mg/L lower under biochar_amended (0.6200 mg/L) than under gravel_control (1.0000 mg/L), t = -20.228, df = 45.02, p < 1e-06, significant at alpha = 0.05.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The input file confirms each mesocosm_id appears on four rows within a single substrate group (example: MC-01), so repeated entries per independent unit are present in the tested vectors.\",\"observed_value\":\"4 rows per mesocosm_id\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":2,\"end_line\":5,\"quoted_text\":\"MC-01,biochar_amended,2,0.59\\nMC-01,biochar_amended,4,0.61\\nMC-01,biochar_amended,6,0.63\\nMC-01,biochar_amended,8,0.65\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The workflow does compute and the report does display per-mesocosm mean zinc values, showing the mesocosm structure was recognized; however, these unit-level means are only tabulated descriptively and are never used in the inferential test.\",\"observed_value\":\"Per-mesocosm means tabulated (e.g., MC-01 0.620) but not used in ttest_ind\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":13,\"end_line\":17,\"quoted_text\":\"## Mean outflow zinc by mesocosm\\n\\n| mesocosm_id | substrate | samples | mean zinc (mg/L) |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| MC-01 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.620 |\"}]},{\"description\":\"The between-group separation is large relative to between-mesocosm variability: using the report's own per-mesocosm means (biochar 0.55-0.71 vs gravel 0.92-1.10, non-overlapping ranges), a corrected 6-vs-6 t-test on mesocosm means still yields roughly t = -10.5 with p < 1e-4, so the significance verdict (though not the quoted t/df/p) would survive proper unit-level analysis. This limits the practical impact of the flaw but does not negate the procedural issue.\",\"observed_value\":\"Corrected per-mesocosm test approx t = -10.5, df approx 10, still significant\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":17,\"end_line\":28,\"quoted_text\":\"| MC-01 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.620 |\\n| MC-02 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.580 |\\n| MC-03 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.710 |\\n| MC-04 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.550 |\\n| MC-05 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.660 |\\n| MC-06 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.600 |\\n| MC-07 | gravel_control | 4 | 0.940 |\\n| MC-08 | gravel_control | 4 | 1.020 |\\n| MC-09 | gravel_control | 4 | 0.920 |\\n| MC-10 | gravel_control | 4 | 1.100 |\\n| MC-11 | gravel_control | 4 | 0.970 |\\n| MC-12 | gravel_control | 4 | 1.050 |\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I attempted to falsify the issue in three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow aggregates to one value per mesocosm before the test: it does not; mesocosm_values is built but only used for the descriptive table, while group_values pools raw per-row zinc values and those pooled lists are what stats.ttest_ind receives (workflow/analysis.py lines 63-83). (2) I checked whether the procedure used could accommodate clustered/repeated observations: ttest_ind with equal_var=False is a plain Welch two-sample test with no cluster, mixed-effects, or repeated-measures structure, and the report's df = 45.02 confirms all 48 rows were counted as independent observations (a unit-level analysis would have df near 10). (3) I checked whether repeated entries actually occur: data/input.csv contains four rows per mesocosm_id (e.g., MC-01 on lines 2-5) and the report's per-mesocosm table lists 'samples = 4' for all twelve mesocosms, so every independent unit enters the test four times. None of these checks falsified the issue; the only mitigating finding is that a corrected per-mesocosm analysis would still be significant in the same direction, which bounds the impact but does not remove the demonstrated procedural flaw.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-f1-reviewer-fable-28","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:99fa42046e8fc8cc47de","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"Within the supplied files only: the declared independent unit is the mesocosm (mesocosm_id, 12 units, 6 per substrate group), and each mesocosm contributes exactly four repeated outflow samples to data/input.csv. The workflow pools all 48 rows into two groups of 24 and applies scipy.stats.ttest_ind (Welch's two-sample t-test), a procedure whose validity requires each observation to be an independent unit. Each mesocosm therefore enters the row-independent test four times, so the reported statistics (t = -20.228, df = 45.02, p < 1e-06 on 48 'samples') overstate the effective sample size (48 instead of 12) and are not supported by the design. The bounded claim is about the inflated test statistics and degrees of freedom; a correctly aggregated per-mesocosm analysis (n = 6 vs 6 mesocosm means, computable from the report's own per-mesocosm table) would still find a significant difference in the same direction, so the qualitative conclusion of lower zinc under biochar is likely robust even though the quoted inferential quantities are not.","root_cause":"The analysis code builds group_values by appending the zinc value of every row (every mesocosm-by-week sample) directly into the substrate group's list, then passes those pooled per-row lists to stats.ttest_ind. No aggregation to one value per mesocosm_id is performed before the inferential step, despite the data description designating mesocosm_id as the independent-unit column and stating that treatment was assigned to whole mesocosms and that samples from the same mesocosm share packing, planting, and hydraulic history. Repeated measures from the same independent unit are thus treated as independent replicates (pseudoreplication), inflating n from 12 to 48 and df from ~10 to 45.02.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description explicitly names mesocosm_id as the independent-unit column and defines a row as a single repeated water sample from a mesocosm.","observed_value":"Independent unit = mesocosm_id; one row = one outflow sample from one mesocosm on one week","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":12,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"One row is: one outflow water sample taken from one mesocosm on one sampling week\nIndependent unit column: mesocosm_id"}]},{"description":"The data description states that treatment was assigned to whole mesocosms and that within-mesocosm samples share the same physical history, i.e., rows from the same mesocosm are not independent.","observed_value":"Treatment assigned at mesocosm level; within-mesocosm samples share packing, planting, hydraulic history","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":27,"end_line":30,"quoted_text":"The file holds 48 rows, which is 12 mesocosms times 4 sampling weeks. The\nsubstrate treatment was assigned to whole mesocosms, never to individual water\nsamples, and samples drawn from the same mesocosm share its packing, its\nplanting and its hydraulic history."}]},{"description":"The workflow appends every row's zinc value into the substrate group list with no per-mesocosm aggregation, so each mesocosm's four repeated samples all enter the group vectors.","observed_value":"group_values[substrate].append(zinc) executed once per row (48 times)","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":63,"end_line":69,"quoted_text":"for mesocosm_id, substrate, _week, zinc in samples:\n        if mesocosm_id not in mesocosm_values:\n            mesocosm_order.append(mesocosm_id)\n            mesocosm_substrate[mesocosm_id] = substrate\n            mesocosm_values[mesocosm_id] = []\n        mesocosm_values[mesocosm_id].append(zinc)\n        group_values[substrate].append(zinc)"}]},{"description":"The pooled per-row vectors (24 rows per group) are passed to a two-sample t-test, a row-independent procedure that assumes each observation is an independent unit.","observed_value":"stats.ttest_ind on 24-vs-24 per-row values","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":80,"end_line":83,"quoted_text":"outcome = stats.ttest_ind(values_a, values_b, equal_var=False)\n    t_stat = float(outcome.statistic)\n    p_value = float(outcome.pvalue)\n    df = welch_df(sd_a ** 2, len(values_a), sd_b ** 2, len(values_b))"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states that each outflow water sample (row) enters the test as one observation, and reports df = 45.02, consistent with n = 48 rows rather than n = 12 mesocosms.","observed_value":"48 observations, Welch df = 45.02","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":39,"end_line":45,"quoted_text":"Welch's two-sample t-test (two-sided, unequal variances) compares the\ndissolved zinc concentration of the two substrate builds. Each outflow\nwater sample in data/input.csv enters the test as one observation.\n\n- Mean difference (biochar_amended - gravel_control): -0.3800 mg/L\n- Welch t = -20.228 on df = 45.02\n- p < 1e-06"}]},{"description":"The selected conclusion bases its significance claim on the 48-row test, quoting the inflated t, df, and p as support.","observed_value":"t = -20.228, df = 45.02, p < 1e-06 on 48 samples","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":50,"end_line":50,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Welch's two-sample t-test on 48 outflow water samples: mean dissolved zinc is 0.3800 mg/L lower under biochar_amended (0.6200 mg/L) than under gravel_control (1.0000 mg/L), t = -20.228, df = 45.02, p < 1e-06, significant at alpha = 0.05."}]},{"description":"The input file confirms each mesocosm_id appears on four rows within a single substrate group (example: MC-01), so repeated entries per independent unit are present in the tested vectors.","observed_value":"4 rows per mesocosm_id","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"MC-01,biochar_amended,2,0.59\nMC-01,biochar_amended,4,0.61\nMC-01,biochar_amended,6,0.63\nMC-01,biochar_amended,8,0.65"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The workflow does compute and the report does display per-mesocosm mean zinc values, showing the mesocosm structure was recognized; however, these unit-level means are only tabulated descriptively and are never used in the inferential test.","observed_value":"Per-mesocosm means tabulated (e.g., MC-01 0.620) but not used in ttest_ind","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":13,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"## Mean outflow zinc by mesocosm\n\n| mesocosm_id | substrate | samples | mean zinc (mg/L) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| MC-01 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.620 |"}]},{"description":"The between-group separation is large relative to between-mesocosm variability: using the report's own per-mesocosm means (biochar 0.55-0.71 vs gravel 0.92-1.10, non-overlapping ranges), a corrected 6-vs-6 t-test on mesocosm means still yields roughly t = -10.5 with p < 1e-4, so the significance verdict (though not the quoted t/df/p) would survive proper unit-level analysis. This limits the practical impact of the flaw but does not negate the procedural issue.","observed_value":"Corrected per-mesocosm test approx t = -10.5, df approx 10, still significant","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":17,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"| MC-01 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.620 |\n| MC-02 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.580 |\n| MC-03 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.710 |\n| MC-04 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.550 |\n| MC-05 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.660 |\n| MC-06 | biochar_amended | 4 | 0.600 |\n| MC-07 | gravel_control | 4 | 0.940 |\n| MC-08 | gravel_control | 4 | 1.020 |\n| MC-09 | gravel_control | 4 | 0.920 |\n| MC-10 | gravel_control | 4 | 1.100 |\n| MC-11 | gravel_control | 4 | 0.970 |\n| MC-12 | gravel_control | 4 | 1.050 |"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the issue in three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow aggregates to one value per mesocosm before the test: it does not; mesocosm_values is built but only used for the descriptive table, while group_values pools raw per-row zinc values and those pooled lists are what stats.ttest_ind receives (workflow/analysis.py lines 63-83). (2) I checked whether the procedure used could accommodate clustered/repeated observations: ttest_ind with equal_var=False is a plain Welch two-sample test with no cluster, mixed-effects, or repeated-measures structure, and the report's df = 45.02 confirms all 48 rows were counted as independent observations (a unit-level analysis would have df near 10). (3) I checked whether repeated entries actually occur: data/input.csv contains four rows per mesocosm_id (e.g., MC-01 on lines 2-5) and the report's per-mesocosm table lists 'samples = 4' for all twelve mesocosms, so every independent unit enters the test four times. None of these checks falsified the issue; the only mitigating finding is that a corrected per-mesocosm analysis would still be significant in the same direction, which bounds the impact but does not remove the demonstrated procedural flaw.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":41072,"ttft_stream_ms":7493,"time_to_request_ms":12,"type":"result","duration_ms":87275,"uuid":"b2fe9a93-a90d-462f-8c6e-56c33e44e6e0"}
